#!/bin/sh
# taskmd entry point. This is the command an adopter types.
#
# It is meant to be found on PATH: the harness appends every enabled plugin's bin/ directory to the
# PATH it hands the agent's shell, so where that holds there is no install step, no PYTHONPATH to
# set and no path to a cache directory anyone has to know. `bin/` is the mechanism; the file name
# is the command.
#
# That is the design and not a guarantee, and this comment used to state it as one - which is the
# worst place for it, because a reader who opens this file has usually got here by the command not
# being found. It has failed on at least one real machine, including the one this project is
# written on: the harness does write the directory into the shell snapshot, and the snapshot's
# `export PATH=` line is truncated mid-value, so sourcing fails and the inherited PATH survives.
# The defect is the harness's and is filed there (T-054); nothing about it is taskmd's to fix.
#
# When it has failed, there is a second way in that does not depend on PATH at all. It is stated
# once, in ../skills/taskmd/SKILL.md, and deliberately not repeated here - two copies of it would
# drift, and the one an adopter is pointed at is that one.
#
# It is a delegate, not a second launcher. Interpreter discovery lives one directory up in
# taskmd.sh and is not repeated here, because the same fact written in two places is the one
# thing this project's design rule forbids - and it is invoked through `sh` rather than executed
# directly so the delegation does not also depend on the target's mode bit.

here=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)
exec sh "$here/../skills/taskmd/taskmd.sh" "$@"
